/*
    SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Copyright (c) Rikarin
    SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

    The rules a utility class cannot say, and nothing else.

    ⚠ Every one of these is here for a reason a class name could not carry: a custom property the
    control library reads, a default for a tag rather than for an element, or a relationship between
    two states. Anything that is "this element, this property, this value" belongs in the markup as
    a utility — a rule here is a rule somebody has to find before they can change it.

    ⚠ **In `@layer components`, and this file is the tree's worked example of why a game's own sheet
    should be.** It used to be unlayered, deliberately: a layer loses to an unlayered rule whatever
    the source order, so these rules beat the generated utilities without `!important` anywhere. That
    is the same defect the engine's own sheets had — `class="p-2"` losing to a rule the author forgot
    they wrote — reproduced one origin up, where nothing the engine does can fix it. A game's sheet is
    `Author` and the engine's whole ladder is `UserAgent`, so this still beats every control default
    without naming a layer; what the layer settles is this sheet's argument with *its own* utilities.

    ⚠ **The statement above the block is not optional.** Layer *order* belongs to whoever names a
    layer first, and this text is concatenated ahead of the generated sheet — so without it
    `components` would be layer zero, `base` and `utilities` would be declared after it, and the
    ladder would come out `components, base, utilities` with `base` in the middle. Naming all three
    here says the order once; the generated sheet restates the same line and a re-declaration never
    moves a layer.
*/

@layer base, components, utilities;

@layer components {

/* ── The controls' own custom properties ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    ProgressBar's fill takes the element's foreground when nothing sets --fill-color, which is what
    lets `text-health` colour a bar. The *track* has no such fallback, so it is set once here rather
    than on every bar in the game.
*/
progress-bar {
    --track-color: #1b1f27;
}

/* ── Defaults per tag, which a utility cannot express ───────────────────────────────────────── */

unit-frame,
action-bar,
quest-tracker,
bag-grid,
vendor-panel,
chat-panel,
loot-roll {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
}

/* ── Relationships between states ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/*
    ⚠ Two classes, so it beats the single-class utility that sets the background — which is the
    whole reason the utility layer exists and the reason this is not written with !important.
    A slot on cooldown is dimmed *and* still shows its icon: hiding it would lose the one thing a
    player is looking at while they wait.
*/
.slot.on-cooldown {
    opacity: 0.45;
}

/*
    A slot the player cannot use at all reads differently from one that is merely counting down.
    Same dimming, plus a border that says "not now" rather than "not yet".
*/
.slot.unusable {
    border-color: #c4453c;
}

/*
    A quest whose objectives are all done is the one line in the tracker a player is looking for.
*/
.quest.ready > quest-title {
    color: #4c9e4c;
}

}
